From c1d3a8d0aa2d61d72da6c11501060eb1650674b6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jeremy Klinger <jtklinger96@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 11:36:30 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] winit/accesskit: incremental tree updates and stable row
 bounds under scroll

Three changes to what a ListView scroll costs the accessibility backend:

1. Freed component ids are parked (1024 deep) before reuse. Reusing one immediately
   made a row created in a frame look like an update of the row destroyed in the
   same frame, raising Name and PositionInSet property-changed events per node.
2. The tree reload is incremental: cached nodes whose place, content and geometry
   are unchanged are neither rebuilt nor re-sent. Each cached node has a content
   tracker and a geometry tracker; a geometry-only change costs a geometry read and
   a compare, and is sent only when the bounds actually moved.
3. Accessible children that sit inside a Flickable are grouped under a synthetic
   GenericContainer node carrying the scroll offset as a transform. Row bounds are
   expressed relative to it, so a scroll changes one node per Flickable instead of
   every visible row. UI Automation sees the same tree (GenericContainer is
   filtered) with the same screen rectangles.

Also exposes ItemRc::map_to_ancestor. Measured with SLINT_ACCESSKIT_STATS=1 on a
40,000-row ListView scrolled at 60 Hz: nodes sent per update fell from ~168 (every
row of the scrolling pane) to ~30-55 (rows entering the viewport plus the content
node); backend CPU per second of scrolling ~15 ms.
